T["07_villa-della-porta-bozzolo"] = dict(title="Villa Della Porta Bozzolo",
clean="""Most villa gardens march straight out from the front door. This one runs off sideways, up the hill, and that's rather the point of it.

From the parterre in front of the villa, turn to face the slope. A stone staircase climbs through four terraces, each with its balustrade and its statues, all the way up to a lawn framed by cypresses near the top of the hill. That lawn is called the theatre, and you're standing in the stalls.

The house began in the sixteenth century as a modest country place for a notary called Giroldino Della Porta, the founder of the family. Two hundred years later, his descendant Gian Angelo the Third married Isabella Giulini, in seventeen fourteen, and the couple decided the old place needed remaking. The terraces date from then. So does the fishpond up at the theatre, from seventeen twenty-three.

Inside, the walls were painted between seventeen twenty-four and seventeen twenty-six. Have a look for the ballroom, where painted landscapes open up the walls as if the room had no walls at all, and the gallery upstairs, full of sirens, nymphs and tritons. Then find the billiard room. It holds a rare example of a game called biribissi, an eighteenth-century gamble that's been described as the ancestor of roulette.

And here's the thing. This was never just a pleasure house. Downstairs and round the back are the working buildings: an ice house, a spinning mill for silk, a cellar of old barrels, and a huge wooden wine press from the sixteenth century. A villa of delight, yes, but one that still had to pay its way.

[beat]

In the late eighteen hundreds the house went to Senator Camillo Bozzolo, a doctor who'd fought with Garibaldi, and in nineteen eighty-nine his heirs gave it to the FAI.

When you've done the rooms, climb all the way up to the theatre and keep going. A path leads on into the woods to a viewpoint over the Valcuvia, with the little fifteenth-century church of San Bernardino close by.""",
respell=[["Della Porta","del la por ta"],["Bozzolo","bot so lo"],["Giroldino","jee rol dee no"],["Gian Angelo","jan an jeh lo"],["Isabella Giulini","isabella joo lee nee"],["biribissi","bee ree bee see"],["Camillo","ka mee lo"],["Garibaldi","gah ree bal dee"],["Valcuvia","val koo vee ah"],["San Bernardino","san bair nar dee no"],FAI],
sources=["https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/villa-della-porta-bozzolo","https://fondoambiente.it/villa-della-porta-bozzolo-inglese/","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Della_Porta_Bozzolo"],
notes=["Bozzolo purchase: FAI says 1884; Wikipedia says 1877. Script says 'late eighteen hundreds'.","'Senator, doctor, Garibaldian' comes from the FAI page.","The axis running parallel to the facade, against the usual rule, is from Wikipedia.","The sixteenth-century wine press is from Wikipedia; its 'largest in Lombardy' claim is uncited, so it isn't used.","FAI says it will be closed from 14 December 2026; check before recording any visiting line."])

T["08_villa-e-collezione-panza"] = dict(title="Villa e Collezione Panza",
clean="""An old aristocratic villa, chandeliers and all, and in the stables, rooms made of nothing but light.

Piazza Litta, on the hill of Biumo above Varese. The long, pale villa in front of you looks exactly like what it is: a country house of the Milanese nobility, with a formal Italian garden in front and an English park beyond, more than thirty thousand square metres of it, looking out over the city.

Its history is a parade of owners. The Menafoglio family built it. In eighteen twenty-three it passed to Pompeo Litta, who had the architect Luigi Canonica enlarge it and turn the farm buildings into stables. In nineteen thirty-five it was bought by Ernesto Panza, and his son Giuseppe grew up here.

Giuseppe Panza became one of the great collectors of his century. From the nineteen fifties until his death in twenty ten he gathered more than two and a half thousand works of contemporary art, much of it American, and much of it about light and colour. Around a hundred and fifty pieces live here now.

Go to the old stables first. That's where you'll find the work of Dan Flavin, who made art out of coloured fluorescent tubes, and this villa has the largest concentration of his work on permanent show anywhere. Alongside him are rooms by James Turrell and Robert Irwin, each one built for this house, each one a kind of small temple to light.

And here's what I love about it. Upstairs, the rooms are still a home: Renaissance wedding chests, nineteenth-century furniture, African and pre-Columbian sculpture. Panza didn't empty the house to make a gallery. He let the eighteenth century and the twentieth sit in the same rooms and argue it out.

[beat]

He and his wife Giovanna gave the villa to the FAI in nineteen ninety-six, and they kept living on the top floor.

Before you leave, walk out into the park and find the greenhouse. Inside it, Sean Scully has replaced the glass with panels of colour, so the plants and the light come through together.""",
respell=[["Panza","pant sa"],["Biumo","bee oo mo"],["Varese","va ray zeh"],["Menafoglio","meh na fol yo"],["Pompeo Litta","pom pay oh lit ta"],["Litta","lit ta"],["Luigi Canonica","loo ee jee ka no nee ka"],["Ernesto","er nes to"],["Giuseppe","joo sep pay"],["Giovanna","jo van na"],["Turrell","tur rel"],["Scully","skully"],FAI],
sources=["https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/villa-e-collezione-panza","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Panza"],
notes=["Construction date conflict: FAI says late sixteenth century, Wikipedia says 1755–1766. The script avoids a date and says only 'the Menafoglio family built it'.","Collecting start: FAI 1955, Wikipedia 1956. Script says 'the nineteen fifties'.","The Flavin 'largest concentration on permanent display' claim is FAI's wording.","No Turrell or Irwin work titles are confirmed, so none are named.","Sean Scully's greenhouse 'Looking Outward' is from Wikipedia only.","Donor name: FAI says Giovanna, Wikipedia says Rosa Giovanna."])

T["09_casa-macchi"] = dict(title="Casa Macchi",
clean="""Somebody left the coffee pot on the stove, and a pipe on the table between the armchairs. The FAI decided to leave them there.

Piazza Sant'Ambrogio, Morazzone, right in the shadow of the church tower. The house in front of you has two floors and a little turret, a typical bit of nineteenth-century Lombard building. It isn't a farmhouse and it isn't a palace. That, as it turns out, is exactly why it matters.

In eighteen ninety-eight it became the property of Adele Bottelli, the wife of Giuseppe Macchi, and the family lived in it for three generations. The last of them was Maria Luisa Macchi, who lived here with her family for most of the twentieth century. In twenty fifteen she left it to the FAI, with its original furniture and a family archive of letters, photographs, diaries and postcards that runs from the Risorgimento to the Second World War.

So most of what's here isn't grand at all. It's the ordinary stuff of a comfortable family in a small village, kept in place down to the smallest detail, as if everyone had just stepped out.

[beat]

And I think that's what's rather brave about it. Heritage usually means the exceptional: the castle, the chapel, the famous painting. This is a museum of the unexceptional, and it turns out that's the thing almost nobody keeps.

Before you go, step into the shop on the square, the Emporio di Casa Macchi. There's been a shop on this piazza since the eighteen hundreds, and until nineteen seventy-five this square and the old main street held twenty-five shops and workshops between them. The Emporio has been fitted out with the counters and shelves of an old Milanese grocer's, and what you buy there helps keep the house going.""",
respell=[["Macchi","mack kee"],["Piazza Sant'Ambrogio","pyat sa san tam bro jo"],["Morazzone","mo rat so neh"],["Adele Bottelli","ah deh leh bot tel lee"],["Giuseppe","joo sep pay"],["Maria Luisa","maria loo ee za"],["Risorgimento","ree sor jee men toh"],["Emporio","em por ee oh"],["piazza","pyat sa"],["Lombard","lom bard"],FAI],
sources=["https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/casa-macchi","https://fondoambiente.it/casa-macchi-eng/","https://fondoambiente.it/il-fai/beni/emporio-macchi/","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casa_Macchi (HTTP 404, no article)"],
notes=["Maria Luisa is Adele's 'nipote' (niece or granddaughter, unclear), so the script avoids the relationship.","The coffee pot on the stove and the pipe on the table are from the FAI page.","The public opening year was not found, so none is stated."])

T["10_monastero-di-torba"] = dict(title="Monastero di Torba",
clean="""This tower was built to keep the barbarians out. Then the barbarians moved in, and after them, the nuns.

Stand in the meadow where you can see the whole group: the tall stone tower, the low church of Santa Maria beside it, and the old monastery buildings with their portico. Behind you is the wooded valley of the Olona.

The tower is the oldest thing here, part of a Roman fort built in the late fourth or fifth century, when this valley was a frontier. It's one of the rare pieces of late Roman military building to survive in the north of Italy. After the Romans came the Goths, the Byzantines and then the Lombards, and each of them used the fort in turn.

In the eighth century, Benedictine nuns arrived and turned the tower into a chapel. Climb to the second floor and you'll find what they painted: Christ blessing, with angels, the Virgin and Child, and along one wall a procession of nuns in prayer. One of the women buried in the tower has a name that's unmistakably Lombard, Aliberga. The church next door has an eighth-century crypt beneath it.

Here's the thing about Torba. It's a place that kept changing its mind about what it was for. Fort, convent, and then, after the nuns left for good in the fourteen eighties and Napoleon closed what remained, a farm. The church became a store for carts. The frescoes vanished under fresh plaster.

[beat]

It was rescued in the nineteen seventies by Giulia Maria Crespi, one of the founders of the FAI, who bought it and gave it to the foundation. Since twenty eleven it's been part of the UNESCO site for the Lombards in Italy, together with Castelseprio up the hill.

Before you go, step into the church to see its newest addition, an altar designed and given by the architect Mario Botta in twenty twenty-five. Then, if you've the time, take the path up through the woods to the ruins of Castelseprio, which is the other half of the story.""",
respell=[["Torba","tor ba"],["Santa Maria","santa maria"],["Olona","oh lo na"],["Aliberga","ah lee bair ga"],["Giulia Maria Crespi","joo lee ah maria kres pee"],["Castelseprio","kas tel seh pree oh"],["Mario Botta","mario bot ta"],["Byzantines","biz an teens"],["UNESCO","you ness co"],FAI],
sources=["https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/monastero-di-torba","https://fondoambiente.it/monastero-di-torba-eng/","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monastero_di_Torba"],
notes=["Acquisition: the FAI body text says 1976, the FAI headline and Wikipedia say 1977. Script says 'the nineteen seventies'.","The tower date is 'fifth to sixth century' in Wikipedia, while the castrum is fourth to fifth. The script ties the date to the fort.","'Aliberga' is Wikipedia's spelling, citing FAI.","The nuns left for Tradate in 1482 (Wikipedia); FAI's 'over seven centuries' was not used.","The recent dedication of the monastery to Crespi was not found in these sources, so it isn't mentioned.","The Botta altar (2025) is from the FAI page."])

T["11_villa-fogazzaro-roi"] = dict(title="Villa Fogazzaro Roi",
clean="""Every Italian schoolchild reads a novel set in this house. The house kept the evidence.

You're in Oria, on the Italian shore of Lake Lugano, about ten kilometres from Lugano itself. The villa sits right on the water, with a hanging garden behind and a little terrace jutting out over the lake.

The writer was Antonio Fogazzaro, from Vicenza, who spent much of his life here. The house came down through his mother's family, and it became the setting for his best-known book, Piccolo mondo antico, The Little World of the Past, published in eighteen ninety-six. It's the story of Franco and Luisa Maironi, and much of it happens in these rooms.

That little terrace with the small cupola and the round iron table is Franco's terrace in the novel. The sweet olive in the hanging garden is in the book too. And the boat landing below the house is where the story reaches its saddest moment, when their little daughter Ombretta drowns.

Have a look for Fogazzaro's writing desk. He wrote notes all over it, some on the very edges of the wood, and one of them records the day he finished the book, the eleventh of August, eighteen ninety-five: finished in tears. That same year his own son Mariano died of typhus, and there's a note on the desk to him as well.

[beat]

What I find rather moving is how little this place pretends. The rooms are small. There's a salon the family called Siberia, because it was impossible to heat. It's a real house that a great writer borrowed for a story, and then the story gave it back to us.

His great-nephew Giuseppe Roi, known as Boso, restored it and gave it to the FAI in two thousand and nine, so that nothing would change after he'd gone. Visits are by guided tour.

When you leave, stand out on the terrace for a moment and look across the water. That's the view Franco looked at, and the view Fogazzaro sat down to describe.""",
respell=[["Fogazzaro","fo gat sa ro"],["Roi","roy"],["Oria","oh ree ah"],["Lugano","loo gah no"],["Vicenza","vee chen tsa"],["Piccolo mondo antico","pee ko lo mon doh an tee ko"],["Maironi","my ro nee"],["Luisa","loo ee za"],["Ombretta","om bret ta"],["Mariano","ma ree ah no"],["Giuseppe","joo sep pay"],["Boso","bo zo"],["Antonio","an toe nee oh"],FAI],
sources=["https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/villa-fogazzaro-roi","https://fondoambiente.it/villa-fogazzaro-roi-eng","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Fogazzaro_Roi"],
notes=["The desk inscription 'Finito nel pianto il Piccolo mondo antico, 11-8-95' and the note to Mariano are from Wikipedia.","The Salone Siberia name comes from Wikipedia.","Whether the characters were based on real people is unverified, so it isn't claimed.","The pergola and a boat are not in the sources, so neither is mentioned.","Guided tour only: per the FAI English page."])

T["12_villa-del-balbianello"] = dict(title="Villa del Balbianello",
clean="""The man who left this villa to Italy had been to the North Pole and up Everest. He asked to end up in the garden.

At the tip of the Lavedo peninsula, find the loggia, the open gallery on the highest point of the garden, with lake on both sides of you. Its columns are smothered in creeping fig, and set into the floor at your feet is an inlaid compass rose.

There was a Franciscan convent on this point from the thirteenth century. Then, at the end of the seventeen hundreds, Cardinal Angelo Maria Durini made it into a retreat for pleasure and for literature, and this loggia was his great idea, with a music room on one side and a library on the other.

The villa went through several owners after him, but the one who matters most arrived in nineteen seventy-four. Guido Monzino was a Milanese businessman and an explorer. He led expeditions to the Arctic, including one to the North Pole in nineteen seventy-one, and in nineteen seventy-three he led the Italian expedition that put climbers on the summit of Everest. Up in the attic is his Museum of Expeditions: flags, photographs, the sledge from the North Pole, and around three hundred small Inuit carvings, the largest collection of Inuit ivories outside Greenland.

Now have a look at the trees. The plane trees by the jetty are pruned into candelabra. And the big holm oak was clipped into an umbrella so that Monzino could see Monte Legnone from his study window. The gardeners still do it, climbing up in harnesses.

[beat]

Monzino died without heirs in nineteen eighty-eight, and left the villa to the FAI. If it looks familiar, that's because film crews love it. It's been a backdrop for Star Wars and for James Bond in Casino Royale.

Before you go, look for the old ice house among the rocks of the park. That's where he asked for his ashes to be laid, which seems about right for a man who spent so much of his life on the ice.""",
respell=[["Balbianello","bal bya nel lo"],["Lavedo","la vay doh"],["Angelo Maria Durini","an jeh lo maria doo ree nee"],["Durini","doo ree nee"],["Guido Monzino","gwee doh mon tsee no"],["Monzino","mon tsee no"],["Inuit","in yoo it"],["Monte Legnone","mon tay len yo nay"],["Franciscan","fran sis kan"],FAI],
sources=["https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/villa-del-balbianello","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_del_Balbianello"],
notes=["Durini's 1787 date is Wikipedia-only; the script says 'end of the seventeen hundreds'.","Everest: FAI says Monzino led it 'primo italiano' to the summit; Wikipedia describes the 1973 expedition he organised. The script says 'led the Italian expedition that put climbers on the summit'.","The candelabra plane trees and the umbrella holm oak are from Wikipedia and FAI.","Films are from Wikipedia.","Arrival routes (boat from Lenno, footpath) were not in the sources, so none are given."])

T["13_la-velarca"] = dict(title="La Velarca",
clean="""It's a house. It's a boat. And it was designed by the same people who gave Milan the Torre Velasca.

On the shore at Ossuccio, look for the long, low boat moored by the bank, with a timber cabin sitting on its deck like a small, very elegant shed. That's La Velarca, and the name is a little joke on the tower.

The hull is older than the house. It belonged to a working lake boat, the Corriera Tremezzina, nineteen metres long, which carried goods and passengers across Lake Como from nineteen eleven. In nineteen fifty-nine, the Milan architects BBPR, Banfi, Belgiojoso, Peressutti and Rogers, set a home on top of it. It was finished in nineteen sixty-one.

The idea came from Fiammetta and Emilio Norsa, who wanted somewhere on the lake to have their friends to stay. And what friends. Gio Ponti, the poet Eugenio Montale, the artist Lucio Fontana, Umberto Eco, and the architects themselves.

Have a look at the round tower of teak in the middle. That's the staircase, wrapped in a cylinder, and it ties the whole thing together. Bedrooms and a sitting room at the bow, a kitchen and bathrooms along the sides, a study and another bedroom at the stern.

[beat]

What I like about it is how unserious it is, in the best way. The same studio was building a skyscraper in the middle of Milan, and on weekends they were fitting out a houseboat for their friends.

The family gave it to the FAI in twenty eleven. The hull was beyond repair, so it was rebuilt at the Riva boatyard, and the interiors were restored from old photographs before it came back to its original mooring here.

If you fancy a walk, the Greenway path along the shore takes you to Villa del Balbianello in about half an hour. It's a lovely way to go from one FAI house to another, by land, past the boats.""",
respell=[["Velarca","veh lar ka"],["Torre Velasca","tor reh veh las ka"],["Ossuccio","oh soo cho"],["Corriera Tremezzina","kor ree air ah treh met see na"],["BBPR","bee bee pee ar"],["Banfi","ban fee"],["Belgiojoso","bel jo yo zo"],["Peressutti","peh reh soo tee"],["Fiammetta","fyam met ta"],["Emilio Norsa","eh mee lee oh nor sa"],["Norsa","nor sa"],["Gio Ponti","jo pon tee"],["Eugenio Montale","eh oo jen ee oh mon tah leh"],["Lucio Fontana","loo cho fon tah na"],["Umberto Eco","oom bair toh ay ko"],["Balbianello","bal bya nel lo"],["Riva","ree va"],FAI],
sources=["https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/la-velarca","https://fondoambiente.it/la-velarca-eng/","https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/beni-fai"],
notes=["Architects are BBPR, not Vico Magistretti.","'The idea came from Fiammetta and Emilio Norsa' follows FAI's wording; no formal commission is stated.","Donated in 2011 by Aldo and Maria Luisa Norsa; restoration began in 2013 at Cantiere Ernesto Riva, Maslianico.","The thirty-minute Greenway walk to Balbianello is from the FAI English page."])

T["14_palazzo-moroni"] = dict(title="Palazzo Moroni",
clean="""From the street it's a fairly sober front on a steep lane. Behind it is the largest private garden in Bergamo's upper town.

You're on Via Porta Dipinta, in the Città Alta. Go in through the entrance and stop in the courtyard of honour. In front of you is a nymphaeum, a grotto-like fountain, and above it the first of the gardens climbing away up the hill.

The palace was built in the middle of the sixteen hundreds by Francesco Moroni, to show the world how far his family had risen, and the Moronis lived in it for almost four centuries.

Climb the grand staircase and look up. The painter Gian Giacomo Barbelli covered it with the tale of Cupid and Psyche, and he went on to paint the main rooms. In one of them, the Hall of the Giants, the figures seem to break straight through the ceiling. Another tells the story of Jerusalem Delivered, the great Renaissance poem of the Crusades.

Then find the Hall of the Golden Age. It holds portraits by Giovan Battista Moroni, one of the finest portrait painters of the Renaissance, and the best known is the Knight in Pink, a young man called Gian Gerolamo Grumelli, signed and dated fifteen sixty. He stands there in rose-coloured silk looking entirely pleased with himself, and frankly he's entitled to.

[beat]

And then go outside, because the garden is the real surprise. Formal terraces climb against the hill of Sant'Eufemia, and above them is the ortaglia, almost two hectares of kitchen garden, meadow and fruit trees, added in the seventeen and eighteen hundreds. A working farm, inside the walls of a hilltop city.

The FAI took on the palace in twenty nineteen, entrusted to it by the foundation that had cared for it.

If you've the legs, climb right up through the ortaglia to the top. From there you look down over the roofs of the Città Alta, and you realise how much green this city has been keeping behind its walls.""",
respell=[["Moroni","mo ro nee"],["Bergamo","bair ga mo"],["Via Porta Dipinta","vee ah por ta dee pin ta"],["Città Alta","chit tah al ta"],["Francesco","fran ches ko"],["Gian Giacomo Barbelli","jan jah ko mo bar bel lee"],["Giovan Battista","jo van bat tees ta"],["Gian Gerolamo Grumelli","jan jeh ro la mo groo mel lee"],["Sant'Eufemia","san teh oo feh mee ah"],["ortaglia","or tal ya"],["nymphaeum","nim fee um"],["Moronis","mo ro neez"],FAI],
sources=["https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/palazzo-moroni","https://fondoambiente.it/palazzo-e-giardini-moroni-eng/","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palazzo_Moroni_(Bergamo) (HTTP 404)"],
notes=["The build date is FAI's 'a metà Seicento'. The 1636 date is unverified and not used.","'Entrusted' follows FAI's 'affidato'; the legal form isn't stated.","The Knight in Pink being 'signed and dated 1560' is from the FAI teaser.","'One of the finest portrait painters of the Renaissance' is a general characterisation of G. B. Moroni, not from the FAI page."])

T["15_castel-grumello"] = dict(title="Castel Grumello",
clean="""Two castles on one rock, and vineyards all the way down.

You're on the rocky spur above Montagna in Valtellina, with the castle ruins around you and the valley opening out below. Behind you rise the Rhaetian Alps. Ahead and beneath, the terraces of vines step down the mountainside.

Look at the ruins and you'll see there are two of them, side by side inside one wall. That's what makes Grumello unusual. It's a twin castle. The eastern half was military, built around a big square tower, with the remains of a second tower for keeping watch. The other half was for living in, with better cut stone and a fireplace, the part meant for comfort and for show.

It was begun at the end of the twelve hundreds or the start of the thirteen hundreds by Corrado de Piro, a Ghibelline who'd come up into the valley with his family after the wars between Milan and Como. It didn't go well for them. Within a few decades they had to sell it to their rivals. And in fifteen twenty-six it was destroyed by the Grey Leagues, the Swiss confederation of the Grisons, along with many of the fortresses of the Valtellina.

[beat]

So what you're looking at is the shell of an argument that was settled five hundred years ago, and the thing that's outlasted it is the farming. The name comes from the knoll it stands on, the grumo, and the terraces below have been growing Nebbiolo, the grape of the Alps, for centuries.

The castle was given to the FAI in nineteen ninety, restored, and opened in two thousand and one.

There's a small restaurant just below the castle, with seasonal food from the valley. If you've the time, stop there, and then walk down among the terraces. You'll see how much patience it takes to farm a mountain, one dry-stone wall at a time.""",
respell=[["Grumello","groo mel lo"],["Montagna in Valtellina","mon tan ya in val tel lee na"],["Valtellina","val tel lee na"],["Rhaetian","ree shun"],["Corrado de Piro","kor rah doh deh pee ro"],["Ghibelline","gib eh line"],["Grisons","gree zon"],["grumo","groo mo"],["Nebbiolo","neb ee oh lo"],["Como","ko mo"],FAI],
sources=["https://fondoambiente.it/luoghi/castel-grumello","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castel_Grumello (HTTP 404)"],
notes=["Donated in 1990 by the company Fedital, not 1987.","The construction date is late thirteenth to early fourteenth century, per FAI.","'Grumello' as a wine name is not on the FAI page, so it isn't used. The Nebbiolo line is from FAI.","Opening hours and prices were not found."])

T["16_villa-carlotta"] = dict(title="Villa Carlotta",
clean="""This villa has had a great many owners, and one wedding present. The wedding present is the one whose name stuck.

From the lakeside gates on the road at Tremezzo, look up. The villa sits above you on its terrace, with the garden spreading out on either side and the lake at your back.

It was built at the end of the sixteen hundreds for the Clerici family of Milan. In eighteen oh one it was bought by Giovanni Battista Sommariva, a politician of Napoleon's Cisalpine Republic and a very serious collector. He's the reason the inside is full of sculpture.

Have a look for three things. Canova's Palamedes, which has stood in its room since eighteen eighteen. A version of Canova's Cupid and Psyche, carved by his pupil Adamo Tadolini. And a long marble frieze by the Danish sculptor Thorvaldsen, The Entry of Alexander the Great into Babylon. Then, in the room named after him, Francesco Hayez's painting Romeo and Juliet's Last Kiss, from eighteen twenty-three, which is about as romantic as paint gets.

Now the name. In eighteen forty-three the villa was bought by Princess Marianne of Prussia, and she gave it to her daughter Charlotte when she married Duke Georg of Saxe-Meiningen. Charlotte died young, in eighteen fifty-five, but the villa kept her name. Carlotta is simply Charlotte in Italian.

[beat]

And here's the thing. Sommariva filled the house, but it was the duke who made the garden. He took a hollow that people called the valley of brambles, ran a stream through it, and turned it into a valley of ferns. The rhododendrons and azaleas came later, and they're what most people come for now.

Since nineteen twenty-seven the villa has been run by a public trust, and it's open to everyone.

If you're here in spring, mid-March to mid-April is when the rhododendrons are at their fullest. Whatever the season, though, find the Valley of Ferns. It's the duke's quiet answer to all that marble.""",
respell=[["Carlotta","kar lot ta"],["Tremezzo","treh met so"],["Clerici","kleh ree chee"],["Giovanni Battista Sommariva","jo van nee bat tees ta som ma ree va"],["Sommariva","som ma ree va"],["Cisalpine","sis al pine"],["Canova","ka no va"],["Palamedes","pa la mee deez"],["Adamo Tadolini","ah dah mo tah doh lee nee"],["Thorvaldsen","tor val sen"],["Francesco Hayez","fran ches ko ah yets"],["Hayez","ah yets"],["Saxe-Meiningen","saxe my ning en"],["Georg","gay org"]],
sources=["https://www.villacarlotta.it/en/about-us/","https://www.villacarlotta.it/en/the-museum/","https://www.villacarlotta.it/en/garden/","https://www.villacarlotta.it/en/visit/","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Carlotta"],
notes=["The Thorvaldsen frieze title is the official one, not 'Triumph of Alexander'.","The Cupid and Psyche replica dates conflict on the official site (1818–20 vs 1819–24), so no date is given.","The gift was made 'on the occasion of the wedding' (official site); 1847 and 1850 are Wikipedia-only, so no year is given.","Ownership: the state took it in 1921 and the Ente Villa Carlotta was recognised in 1927. The script says 'since 1927, a public trust'."])

T["17_ponte-san-michele"] = dict(title="Ponte San Michele",
clean="""Eighty-five metres above the river, the trains run underneath the cars.

From the bank of the Adda, or from the walkway on the bridge itself, take in the whole span. One great iron arch leaps the gorge between Paderno and Calusco, and the deck sits on top of it like a long straight shelf. Look closely and you'll see there are two decks. The road runs along the top, with a footpath either side. The railway runs through the level below.

It was built between eighteen eighty-seven and eighteen eighty-nine by the Società Nazionale delle Officine di Savigliano, to a design by their head engineer, a Swiss called Jules Röthlisberger. It's two hundred and sixty-six metres long. The arch spans a hundred and fifty. About four hundred and seventy men put it together from around two and a half thousand tonnes of iron, all of it riveted. There isn't a weld anywhere.

If that sounds like something Gustave Eiffel might have done, you're not wrong. It belongs to exactly the same moment as his great iron bridges in France and Portugal, and as the Eiffel Tower itself.

[beat]

And here's the thing worth knowing. This isn't a monument you look at. It's a working bridge, well over a hundred and thirty years old, still carrying a railway line. In September twenty eighteen it was closed overnight after sensors picked up worrying readings. Walkers were back in March twenty nineteen, cars that November, and trains in September twenty twenty.

There was a plan to put it forward for UNESCO alongside its sister bridges abroad, but it was dropped in twenty twenty-four, because a new bridge is planned right next to this one. Enjoy it while it stands alone.

From here, the paths along the Adda are the Ecomuseo Adda di Leonardo, the river Leonardo da Vinci knew. Follow the river a few kilometres upstream and you'll come to Imbersago, and a ferry that still crosses the Adda with no engine at all.""",
respell=[["Ponte San Michele","pon tay san mee kay lay"],["Adda","ad da"],["Paderno","pa dair no"],["Calusco","ka loos ko"],["Società Nazionale delle Officine di Savigliano","so chee eh tah nat syo nah leh del leh of fee chee neh dee sa vee lyah no"],["Jules Röthlisberger","zhool rert lis bair ger"],["Gustave Eiffel","goo stav eye fel"],["Eiffel","eye fel"],["Ecomuseo Adda di Leonardo","eh ko moo zeh oh ad da dee lay oh nar doh"],["Imbersago","im bair sah go"],["UNESCO","you ness co"]],
sources=["https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ponte_San_Michele","https://www.parcoaddanord.it/"],
notes=["Closure: Wikipedia gives both the 14 and 15 September 2018. The script says 'September twenty eighteen'.","Builder name: Wikipedia says 'Società Nazionale Officine di Savigliano'; the script uses the common form with 'delle'. Use Wikipedia's form if strictness matters.","Leonardo isn't linked to the bridge itself. The script refers only to the Ecomuseo's name and the river.","The 'largest arch bridge in the world when built' claim is uncited on Wikipedia, so it isn't used.","The distance from the bridge to Imbersago ('a few kilometres upstream') should be checked on a map."])

T["18_villa-reale-di-monza"] = dict(title="Villa Reale di Monza",
clean="""Around seven hundred rooms, and the architect was given three years to finish them.

Stand in the great courtyard in front of the main facade. The palace wraps round you in a long U, pale and calm, with the town of Monza behind you and the park beyond the building.

In seventeen seventy-seven the commission went to the imperial architect Giuseppe Piermarini. The client was the Archduke Ferdinand of Habsburg, son of the Empress Maria Theresa and governor of Milan, who wanted a summer residence in the country. Piermarini delivered it in about three years.

Then the regimes changed and the palace changed with them. Napoleon's stepson, Eugène de Beauharnais, made it his residence as Viceroy of Italy, and that's when it became the Villa Reale, the Royal Villa. The vast park behind it was founded by decree of Napoleon himself in eighteen oh five. It's seven hundred and twenty hectares, one of the largest walled parks in Europe, and somewhere in it is the Formula One circuit.

After unification it became the favourite house of King Umberto the First. And this is where the story turns. On the twenty-ninth of July, nineteen hundred, Umberto was shot dead in Monza by the anarchist Gaetano Bresci.

[beat]

His son never forgave the place. Victor Emmanuel the Third closed the villa, and much of its furniture went off to Rome. It stayed shut, more or less, for a century, until the restoration that reopened it in twenty fourteen.

Inside, look for the royal apartments: the King's armoury and study, and Queen Margherita's sitting room and boudoir, and the billiard room with its tables by the cabinetmaker Giuseppe Maggiolini.

When you come out, walk round to the rose garden, with about a thousand varieties, and then keep going into the park. On a weekday morning you can walk for an hour and hardly see another soul.""",
respell=[["Villa Reale","villa reh ah leh"],["Monza","mon tsa"],["Giuseppe Piermarini","joo sep pay pyair ma ree nee"],["Piermarini","pyair ma ree nee"],["La Scala","la ska la"],["Habsburg","haps burg"],["Eugène de Beauharnais","er zhen deh bo ar nay"],["Umberto","oom bair toh"],["Gaetano Bresci","guy eh tah no bresh ee"],["Margherita","mar geh ree ta"],["Giuseppe Maggiolini","joo sep pay mad jo lee nee"]],
sources=["https://reggiadimonza.it/storia-della-villa-reale/","https://reggiadimonza.it/villa-reale-cosa-vedere/","https://reggiadimonza.it/parco/","https://reggiadimonza.it/informazioni-utili/","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Villa_Reale_(Monza)"],
notes=["'Around seven hundred rooms' is from Wikipedia only (700 rooms, 22,000 m²).","Maria Theresa as commissioner is from Wikipedia; the official site says Ferdinand was her son.","The Expiatory Chapel was not in the sources, so it isn't mentioned.","'Stayed shut, more or less, for a century' is a simplification. Restoration was 2012–14, and the official ceremony was 26 June 2014."])

T["19_san-siro-leonardos-horse"] = dict(title="Ippodromo San Siro and Leonardo's Horse",
clean="""Leonardo da Vinci spent years on a horse that was never cast. Five hundred years later, somebody finished the job.

Piazzale dello Sport, outside the racecourse at San Siro. In front of you, on its plinth, is a bronze horse a little over seven metres tall, caught mid-stride.

In fourteen eighty-two Ludovico Sforza, Il Moro, Duke of Milan, asked Leonardo to make the largest equestrian statue in the world, in honour of his father, Francesco Sforza. Leonardo worked on it for years, and in fourteen ninety-three a full-size clay model went on show in Milan. The bronze was set aside to cast it. Then war came. The metal went to make cannon, and when the French took the city in fourteen ninety-nine, their soldiers used the clay horse for target practice until there was nothing left.

That might have been the end of it. But in nineteen seventy-seven an American called Charles Dent set out to make it. He spent years raising money and died in nineteen ninety-four before it was done. The supermarket owner Frederik Meijer took on the cost, on one condition: there would be two horses, one for Milan and one for his gardens in Michigan. The sculptor Nina Akamu shaped it, and the horse was unveiled here in September nineteen ninety-nine.

[beat]

So it's not quite Leonardo's horse, and it never could be. It's a promise kept by strangers, five centuries late, and I think that's rather lovely.

The racecourse behind it opened in nineteen twenty, designed by Paolo Vietti-Violi, and its buildings are full of Liberty-style detail. The area around the horse, and the small gallery about the racecourse, are free to visit every day.

If you've the time, follow the self-guided trail round the grounds. There are seven markers to find, and a botanical park with more than seventy species of trees. Look out for the Palazzina del Peso, the old weighing house, one of the original Liberty buildings.""",
respell=[["Ippodromo","ip po droh mo"],["San Siro","san see ro"],["Piazzale dello Sport","pyat sah leh del lo sport"],["Ludovico Sforza","loo doh vee ko sfort sa"],["Sforza","sfort sa"],["Il Moro","eel mo ro"],["Francesco","fran ches ko"],["Frederik Meijer","fred er ick my er"],["Nina Akamu","nina ah kah moo"],["Paolo Vietti-Violi","pow lo vee et ee vee oh lee"],["Palazzina del Peso","pa lat see na del pay zo"]],
sources=["https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ippodromo_del_galoppo_di_San_Siro","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cavallo_di_Leonardo","https://www.ippodromisnai.it/il-cavallo-di-leonardo/","https://www.ippodromisnai.it/tour-scoprisansiro/","https://www.ippodromisnai.it/info-utili/"],
notes=["Height: the official site says 'a little over seven metres'; the 7.2 m figure isn't used.","The French and the crossbows: the official site hedges with 'sembra che'; Wikipedia is firmer.","Free access to the horse and the GAMI gallery, 10:00–19:00, is from the official site."])

T["20_traghetto-di-leonardo"] = dict(title="Traghetto di Leonardo",
clean="""No engine, no fuel, and it's been taking people across this river for centuries.

At the landing in Imbersago, look out at the Adda. Stretched right across the water is a steel cable, and hanging from it is a wide wooden ferry, flat-decked, with a single ferryman aboard. On the far bank is Villa d'Adda, in another province.

Here's how it works. The ferry is tied to the cable, and the ferryman angles the boat against the current with its rudder. The river pushes on one side of the hull and simply shoves it across. He uses an iron pole to push off. It takes about five minutes, and it works because the river never stops.

It's called Leonardo's ferry, and he did know this river well. He stayed downstream at Vaprio d'Adda with the Melzi family in the early fifteen hundreds, and there's a drawing of his, now in the Royal Collection at Windsor, of the Adda with a ferry tied to a cable. But to be honest, he didn't invent it. Ferries like this were common all along the river, and there were five on the Adda at one time. This is the one that survived.

[beat]

What's documented is that a cable crossing ran here by sixteen seventy-eight. The boat you're looking at is younger. The old one was taken out of service as unsafe in nineteen ninety-one, and Venetian shipwrights built an exact copy, which began crossing in May nineteen ninety-four. Pope John the Twenty-Third used this ferry on his pilgrimages to the sanctuary up the hill.

Since the bridge at Brivio closed for works in twenty twenty-six, the ferry has gone back to being what it always was, a way to get to work.

So take the crossing, if it's running. Then, on the Imbersago side, walk up to the Santuario della Madonna del Bosco, the sanctuary the Pope was making for. It's worth the climb.""",
respell=[["Traghetto di Leonardo","tra get toh dee lay oh nar doh"],["Imbersago","im bair sah go"],["Adda","ad da"],["Villa d'Adda","villa dad da"],["Vaprio d'Adda","vah pree oh dad da"],["Melzi","mel tsee"],["Brivio","bree vee oh"],["Santuario della Madonna del Bosco","san too ah ree oh del la ma don na del bos ko"]],
sources=["https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traghetto_di_Leonardo","https://es.euronews.com/culture/2026/06/10/en-lombardia-el-transbordador-de-leonardo-transporta-pasajeros-desde-hace-500-anos","https://www.in-lombardia.it/en/event/traghetto-leonardesco","https://www.comune.imbersago.lc.it (HTTP 503, not read)"],
notes=["Leonardo's stay at Vaprio (1506–07) and the Windsor drawing are from Wikipedia and Euronews; both say he didn't invent the ferry.","Wikipedia ties the Windsor drawing to the Vaprio/Canonica crossing. The script says only 'a ferry tied to a cable'.","The current boat: the old one was blocked in December 1991 and the replica was inaugurated on 22 May 1994 (Wikipedia).","The operator conflicts (municipal concession vs volunteers), so the operator isn't stated.","Weekday running since the Brivio bridge closed in May 2026 is from Euronews; check it before recording.","The Luoghi del Cuore link is unverified, so it isn't mentioned."])

T["21_rocca-di-lonato"] = dict(title="Rocca di Lonato and Casa del Podestà",
clean="""A senator bought a castle, a house and fifty thousand books, and then gave the lot away.

Up on the walls of the Rocca, look north-east. The fortress runs along a ridge of glacial moraine, almost a hundred and eighty metres long, and from the wall walk you can see out over the southern end of Lake Garda.

People have been fortifying this ridge since the tenth century. It passed from the lords of Verona to the Visconti of Milan, then the Gonzaga, and in fourteen forty-one to the Republic of Venice, which held Lonato for more than three hundred and fifty years. You'll notice it's really two castles in one: the Rocchetta at the top, and the larger main quarter below. Up in the Rocchetta, there's now a museum of birds, with around seven hundred specimens.

Now look down into the town, towards a house just below the fortress. That's the Casa del Podestà, built in the middle of the fourteen hundreds for the official who governed Lonato on behalf of Venice.

[beat]

Here's where the senator comes in. Ugo Da Como was a lawyer from Brescia, then a member of parliament, then a senator. In nineteen oh six he bought the Casa del Podestà at public auction, and had it restored by the leading architect of Brescia, Antonio Tagliaferri. Later he bought the Rocca too. And into the house went his library: around fifty thousand volumes, four hundred incunabula, the earliest printed books, and five hundred manuscripts going back to the twelfth century.

He lived there until his death in nineteen forty-one, and his foundation was created by royal decree the following year, so that none of it would be broken up. It's still a private foundation, and still open to the public.

When you come down from the walls, go into the Casa del Podestà and ask to see the library. It's one man's lifetime of reading, still on the shelves where he left it.""",
respell=[["Rocca di Lonato","rock ka dee lo nah toh"],["Rocca","rock ka"],["Lonato","lo nah toh"],["Casa del Podestà","kah za del po deh stah"],["Garda","gar da"],["Visconti","vis kon tee"],["Gonzaga","gon zah ga"],["Rocchetta","rock ket ta"],["Ugo Da Como","oo go da ko mo"],["Brescia","bresh ya"],["Antonio Tagliaferri","an toe nee oh tal ya fair ree"],["incunabula","in kew nab yoo la"],["Verona","veh ro na"]],
sources=["https://www.fondazioneugodacomo.it/it/fondazione","https://www.fondazioneugodacomo.it/it/castello-rocca","https://www.fondazioneugodacomo.it/it/museo-casa-del-podesta","https://www.fondazioneugodacomo.it/it/biblioteca-archivio","https://www.fondazioneugodacomo.it/it/orari-di-apertura-e-biglietteria","https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rocca_di_Lonato"],
notes=["The tenth-century origin is Wikipedia-only.","'Lords of Verona' are the Scaligeri, per Wikipedia.","Casa del Podestà purchase: official 1906, Wikipedia 1909. The script uses 1906.","The bird museum location is the Casa del Capitano per the official site; the script says 'up in the Rocchetta'.","'Look north-east' for Lake Garda is my orientation; check it on site.","Winter hours: the Rocca opens weekends only from November to February."])

